WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:05.360
Episode 4 of Loki was the most revealing&nbsp;
and game-changing installment yet.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.360 --> 00:00:10.280
The episode also had some treats&nbsp;
in store for Marvel Comics fans and&nbsp;MCU diehards. 

00:00:10.280 --> 00:00:14.560
Here are a few Easter eggs&nbsp;
you may have missed in Loki Episode 4.

00:00:14.560 --> 00:00:19.973
One of the biggest surprises to come in&nbsp;
Episode 4 is the return of Jaimie Alexander as Lady Sif. &nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.973 --> 00:00:25.440
We hadn't seen the character in the MCU&nbsp;
since 2013's "Thor: The Dark World," and while we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:30.160
suspected the missing hero might be returning,&nbsp;
her appearance was still shocking nonetheless.

00:00:30.160 --> 00:00:35.440
Sif's cameo was brief, and those unfamiliar with&nbsp;
Norse mythology might have missed a famous story&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.440 --> 00:00:40.720
hiding in plain sight. When she first encounters&nbsp;
Loki, Sif is holding a lock of her dark hair.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.720 --> 00:00:46.160
She charges at Loki, beyond aggravated that he&nbsp;
cut off her tresses, and calls him "pathetic"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.160 --> 00:00:51.840
and "conniving" in addition to other unpleasant&nbsp;
words. She aggressively knees and punches him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.840 --> 00:00:56.560
and Loki has to relive the event over&nbsp;
and over as he's stuck in a time loop.

00:00:56.560 --> 00:00:59.840
While it might seem like a storyline&nbsp;
the "Loki" writers conjured up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.840 --> 00:01:04.240
they actually pulled it directly from the two&nbsp;
character's mythologies. In the Norse myths,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.240 --> 00:01:10.400
unlike the MCU, Sif is known for her long golden&nbsp;
hair. In the myths, Loki cuts off Sif's hair,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.400 --> 00:01:16.000
which puts Thor - her husband - in a fit of rage.&nbsp;
After his brother charges him with replacing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:20.960
her hair, Loki travels to Nidavellir - where&nbsp;
Stormbreaker was forged in Infinity War - so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.960 --> 00:01:26.653
the dwarves can create new hair for Sif out of&nbsp;
gold which would grow once placed on her head.

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:32.240
We finally get a look at the Time-Keepers in&nbsp;
Episode 4 after their appearances were teased&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.240 --> 00:01:37.600
with TVA sculptures throughout earlier episodes&nbsp;
of the series. When Loki and Sylvie are brought&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.600 --> 00:01:42.000
before them to be pruned, the Time-Keepers&nbsp;
give them a chance to speak. We don't get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:46.960
much out of these mysterious beings, although&nbsp;
their disdain for the Loki variants is obvious.

00:01:46.960 --> 00:01:52.800
With the help of Hunter B-15, who's had her&nbsp;
memories of her pre-TVA life restored by Sylvie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:57.760
the Lokis are able to battle and defeat&nbsp;
their captors, and Sylvie severs the head&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.760 --> 00:02:02.800
of a Time-Keeper - only to discover that&nbsp;
they're just androids. Beyond disappointed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.800 --> 00:02:08.400
Loki and Sylvie are left wondering who created&nbsp;
the TVA. Marvel Comics might have some answers.

00:02:08.400 --> 00:02:15.280
The Time-Keepers first appeared in 1979's "Thor"&nbsp;
#282 and were created by He Who Remains - someone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.280 --> 00:02:21.120
unmentioned during Episode 4. Also known as&nbsp;
Him Who Remains - or more simply Him - this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.120 --> 00:02:27.613
mystery being was the last director of the TVA,&nbsp;
who put the Time-Keepers in place to oversee&nbsp;the organization. 

00:02:27.613 --> 00:02:32.800
Originally, Him created&nbsp;
the Time-Twisters in 1976's "Thor" #243,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.800 --> 00:02:36.800
another trio of beings who strayed from&nbsp;
their directive and began destroying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.800 --> 00:02:41.280
Earth every 3000 years. In "Loki,"&nbsp;
the time-looping devices used by the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.280 --> 00:02:45.840
TVA are known as "Time-Twisters," in&nbsp;
an apparent nod to these characters.

00:02:50.401 --> 00:02:53.360
"Sorry, the Time Twister just&nbsp;
loops you, not the furniture."

00:02:53.360 --> 00:02:57.520
While we don't know who specifically&nbsp;
created the TVA in the world of "Loki,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.520 --> 00:03:00.907
He Who Remains - or the Time-Twisters&nbsp;
- might have some insight.

00:03:01.280 --> 00:03:05.760
Different versions of Loki are teased in&nbsp;
Episode 2 during a briefing Mobius gives&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.760 --> 00:03:11.040
to a group of Minutemen and other TVA workers&nbsp;
before they take off to Oshkosh, Wisconsin.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.040 --> 00:03:16.160
These variants are displayed on a hologram, which&nbsp;
depicts five different versions of the character.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.160 --> 00:03:21.680
None of these guys, however, match the four new&nbsp;
Lokis we meet in the Episode 4 mid-credits scene.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.680 --> 00:03:25.040
Thanks to the credits, we know&nbsp;
these variants are Boastful Loki,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.040 --> 00:03:29.760
Classic Loki, and Kid Loki. Oh&nbsp;
yeah: There's also a crocodile Loki!

00:03:29.760 --> 00:03:33.680
Classic Loki sports the traditional garb&nbsp;
the character wore when he first appeared in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.680 --> 00:03:39.680
1962's "Journey Into Mystery" #85. Kid Loki&nbsp;
has a much shorter history in the comics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.680 --> 00:03:46.080
first appearing in 2010's "Thor" #617. This&nbsp;
incarnation came about after the original Loki&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.080 --> 00:03:50.640
died, but he was reborn as a child who&nbsp;
eventually joins the Young Avengers.

00:03:50.640 --> 00:03:55.280
The backgrounds on our other two variants&nbsp;
are vague, but Boastful Loki's possession&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.280 --> 00:04:01.200
of a hammer could be a nod to 2020's "Thor" #4.&nbsp;
In this comic, Thor's brother is able to lift&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.200 --> 00:04:07.360
Mjolnir while on Jotunheim. Loki was also able to&nbsp;
lift Mjolnir during Marvel's AXIS event, when his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.360 --> 00:04:13.520
moral alignment was reversed, which made him the&nbsp;
God of Heroism. Comic precedents aside, Boastful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.520 --> 00:04:18.720
Loki's appearance at least hints at some sort&nbsp;
of confirmation that a Loki variant is worthy.

00:04:18.720 --> 00:04:23.840
As far as the crocodile goes, there seems to be&nbsp;
no history of this character in the comics. We've&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.840 --> 00:04:31.253
seen a frog version of Thor before, a.k.a. Throg&nbsp;
the Frog of Thunder, in 2009's "Lockjaw and the&nbsp;Pet Avengers" #1. &nbsp;

00:04:31.253 --> 00:04:38.560
And Loki turns Thor into a frog&nbsp;
in "Thor" #363, so hey, why not a crocodile Loki?

00:04:38.560 --> 00:04:43.760
After Loki gets pruned, he winds up in New York&nbsp;
City, where he meets his new variant buddies.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.760 --> 00:04:47.760
Most of the focus in this scene is on the&nbsp;
new Lokis, and while your eyes are drawn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.760 --> 00:04:52.773
to a crocodile wearing gold horns, there's&nbsp;
something major you might have missed in the&nbsp;background. &nbsp;

00:04:52.773 --> 00:04:57.040
Avengers Tower - previously known&nbsp;
as Stark Tower - is looming in the distance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.040 --> 00:05:02.080
absolutely destroyed. We've seen the building&nbsp;
take a beating before, but nothing like this.

00:05:02.080 --> 00:05:05.760
The new Lokis appear to be hiding&nbsp;
out in another apocalyptic event,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.760 --> 00:05:10.080
although this time, it seems to have already&nbsp;
happened, as opposed to previous episodes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.080 --> 00:05:14.240
when the events were imminent. Avengers&nbsp;
Tower is decimated in this timeline,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.240 --> 00:05:19.440
with its top floor balconies obliterated as it&nbsp;
rests on a tilt. It looks like the Barn Door&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.440 --> 00:05:26.480
Protocol wasn't installed on this former superhero&nbsp;
hangout, like it was for Avengers HQ in Endgame.

